// What vision's types cost the checker — which is one clause, and the clause cannot be a `bool`.
//
// `packages/wacc/src/check.wac` is 10,539 lines and nothing here had predicted anything about it.
// This file is about forty of them: `assignable(C c, string want, string got)`, which 26 call sites
// share and which is where every widening in the language lives.
//
// **The premise I started with was wrong and worth recording.** The file's header says a returned
// name must have the declared type *exactly*, so I expected `union<A, B>` to be *subtyping added to a
// language that has none*. It is not: `assignable` already has five clauses — nullable widening both
// ways, nullable-into-base, struct inheritance, a generic child into a parent, and a variant into its
// enum — and the header's *exactly* is about rung 3's slice rather than about the language. The
// relation exists. Vision adds a clause to it.
//
// And every clause there carries the miss it was added for. *"reported, in every position a value can
// be written: local, parameter, return, field, array element and a later assignment, all six measured
// against the reference"*; *"the sweep found it forty times over"*; *"missed because nothing in the
// spec corpus assigns a child to a parent"*. So the honest cost of a sixth clause is not the clause —
// it is that the five before it each took a measured miss to find, and nothing suggests the sixth is
// different.

import { Tok, Ty } from "./ast.wac";

/**
 * Whether a value of type `got` may be where a `want` is expected — **the sixth clause**.
 *
 * This said **subset**: `union<A, B>` accepts `A`, accepts `union<A>`, and does not accept
 * `union<A, C>`. `../../../TECHNICAL.md` settles that it is wrong, and it settles it with a
 * measurement rather than an argument — the lowering exists and was run:
 *
 * > `union<A, B>` lowers to an enum of one-field variants … **Injection is implicit.** A value of a
 * > member type, in a slot whose type is the union, is wrapped … **This is the whole of what the
 * > language adds.**
 *
 * An enum of one variant and an enum of two are different types, so `union<A>` is not a `union<A, B>`
 * at run time and cannot be at compile time either. And the lowering **nests rather than flattens**,
 * deliberately — `union<SourceFailed, Corrupt>` becomes an enum whose variant carries `Corrupt`'s
 * enum, which is what makes `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac`'s one-arm `Err(is Corrupt):` possible and
 * what flattening would destroy.
 *
 * So the relation is **nominal membership**, not subset: `union<A, Corrupt>` accepts a `Corrupt`
 * because `Corrupt` is *a declared member*, not because its members are contained. One lookup —
 * `c.isUnionMember(want, got)` — with no set comparison, no canonicalisation and no sorting.
 */
export bool assignable(const Ctx c, Ty want, Ty got) { … }

/** The context a check runs in: what is a struct, what is an enum, what a name resolves to. */
export struct Ctx { … }

/*
 * ## And `never` needs no clause at all, which is the one place vision makes this function smaller
 *
 * `never` is the **empty union** — an enum with no variants, so no value of it exists.
 *
 * **That is still the definition and it no longer makes the function smaller**, which the first
 * version of this file claimed. Under a *subset* rule `{} ⊆ S` gave assignable-to-everything for
 * free; under nominal membership it does not, and `never` needs a clause of its own. The clause is
 * one line and vacuously true — a value that cannot exist can go anywhere — but it is a line, and
 * the saving was an artefact of the wrong relation.
 *
 * `../../../QUESTIONS.md` records `never` as *a type with non-trivial semantics, asserted in one doc
 * comment*. This is the semantics: not *assignable to everything* as a special case, but *the union
 * of nothing*, from which that follows. It also settles what `union<never, E>` reduces to —
 * `{} ∪ S = S`, no rule needed — which that entry lists as an open operation, and it settles
 * `@/packages/wacc/src/genlower.wac`'s *the `Waiting` arm has to be deleted*: a `match` over
 * `Step<never, Y, R>` has two arms because the third's payload type is the empty union and no value
 * of it exists.
 *
 * Three open questions, one definition, and the definition is a sentence rather than a rule. Worth
 * noticing that it is not written down anywhere: `never` appears in seven places in this directory
 * and none of them says *the empty union*.
 */

/**
 * Why `try` cannot use the function above, and this is the finding.
 *
 * `try f()` inside a function declared to fail with `E2` is legal exactly when the callee's error
 * union `E1` is a subset of `E2` — the same relation, asked the same way round. And when it is not,
 * the checker has to say **which member** is missing:
 *
 *     try: `parseProxy` can fail with `PortNotDigits`, which this function does not declare
 *
 * `assignable` answers `bool`. It has never needed to say *why not*, because none of its five clauses
 * can fail **partially**: a `string` is not an `i32` and there is nothing to enumerate. A union is the
 * first relation in this language where failure has a *witness*, and the witness is what a caller
 * needs to fix it.
 */
export Ty? missingFrom(const Ctx c, Ty want, Ty got) { … }

// ## 1. A predicate is the wrong shape and this directory has counted the shape before
//
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md`: **78 `bool` functions collapse 405 refusals, and 17 of them are
// verifications.** `assignable` is the 79th and it is not in that count, because it is not a
// verification — it is a *relation*, and relations that can fail in more than one way have the same
// problem for the same reason.
//
// The difference from the seventeen is what makes it worth its own note. A verifier that answers
// `bool` throws away *which rule refused*, and the fix is an enum of rules. A **relation** that
// answers `bool` throws away *which element*, and the fix is not an enum — the set of possible
// answers is the members of a type the checker was handed. So `Ty? missingFrom(…)` rather than
// `Reason refused(…)`, and the two look alike and are not.
//
// **`assignable` stays a `bool` and gains a sibling.** Twenty-six call sites want the predicate and
// one wants the witness, and making all twenty-six unwrap an option to ask *may this go here* would
// be the tail wagging the dog. What the language has no way to say is that the two agree —
// `missingFrom(c, w, g) is null` **iff** `assignable(c, w, g)` — which is a law, and the fourth in
// this directory after `hash`/`eq`, `combine`'s associativity, and `Key`'s.
//
// ## 2. What the sixth clause does not cost
//
// Three things I expected to pay for and do not:
//
//   * **Ordering.** Under nominal membership nothing compares member lists at all, so there is
//     nothing to canonicalise — and `@/packages/http`'s `ResponseFault = union<RequestFault,
//     BadStatus>` **nests** rather than flattening, which is the lowering's own decision and the
//     reason `Err(is RequestFault):` is one arm.
//   * **`never` as a special case** — §above.
//   * **Nullable interaction.** The existing clauses recurse, so `union<A, B>?` falls out. That is a
//     property of how that function was written and not of unions, and it is worth noticing: a
//     cascade of `if`s that each recurse composes with a new clause, where a `switch` on a pair of
//     type kinds would have needed a row per combination.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **The `Ty`-is-a-string worry was wrong, and reading the reason gave the answer.** This section
// first said the checker throws a type tree away and that union subset therefore means parsing
// `union<A, B>` on every comparison. Both halves are off.
//
// The AST *does* have a tree — `packages/wacc/src/ast.wac` declares `struct Ty` with `Arr(Ty elem)`,
// `Nullable(Ty inner)`, `Funcref(Ty ret, Ty[] params)` — and `typeOfTy` renders it to a name **on
// purpose**, with the reason in the file:
//
// > A generic instantiation spells itself. `Box<i32>` and `Box<f64>` are different types and the
// > model here is that a type *is* its canonical name, so the name has to carry the arguments …
// > **Invariance then costs nothing: two instantiations differ exactly when their names do.**
//
// That is a model rather than a shortcut, and the five existing clauses live inside it comfortably:
// identity is string equality, and *assignability* is a relation over names computed by asking the
// context — `c.isStruct(want)`, `descendsFrom(c, got, want)` — or by small string surgery,
// `withoutNull`.
//
// So the real question is not *tree or string*. It is **which of those two a new clause needs**, and
// the answer for unions is the first one:
//
//   * `withoutNull` works because `?` is a suffix. A union's members are a *set*, and no amount of
//     surgery on `"union<A, B>"` is cheaper than parsing it.
//   * But a union is **declared**, exactly as a struct is, so the context can hold it:
//     `c.unionMembers(name)` is a lookup and `descendsFrom` is the precedent. The sixth clause asks
//     `c` a question, which is what four of the five before it do.
//
// **So the clause is a lookup, not a parse, and the cost is one table** — the same table structs
// already have. Canonicalising a union's name (members sorted, deduplicated) then makes equality free
// as it is for everything else, and only the subset test consults the table.
//
// The thing worth keeping from the wrong version: the *cost of a vision feature is decided by a
// representation choice made years earlier for another reason*. That is still true here. It just
// happens that the choice — a type is its canonical name, and the context answers questions about
// names — is the one that makes this clause cheap rather than the one that makes it expensive.
//
// **Nothing says the checker's relation and the runtime's are the same.** `assignable` decides what
// compiles; `is` decides what a match arm takes at run time; and a union's arms are matched by type.
// If the two disagree about `union<A, B>` accepting `union<A>`, a program compiles and takes the wrong
// arm. The shipped language keeps them in step by having one notion of a name; a subset relation is
// the first place they could drift, and the only thing that would catch it is a test that asks both.
